annote = {This study utilizes all four phases from the NICHD SECCYD longitudinal study (ages 54 months to 15years) to investigate possible mechanisms between neighborhood advantages and children's academic outcomes. To get a comprehensive scope of the child's environment, the analyses included hierarchical linear models that examined neighborhood socioeconomic advantage, quality of home learning environment, quality of child care centers, quality of classroom instruction, and individual child and family characteristics. The results found that children living in advantaged neighborhoods with higher socioeconimic factors are exposed to higher quality environments that positively influence their educational outcomes. Researchers provide details on the strength and shape of the association.},
