abstract = {Five experiments involving 447 undergraduates used a self-probe methodology in both simulated and real conditions to demonstrate that individuals do engage in spontaneous attributional search. Results show that this search is most likely when the outcome of an event is negative and unexpected. Content analysis of attributional questions also suggests that causal search is biased toward internality after failure but toward externality following success. Data reveal that the most commonly used heuristic in attributional search is to center on the locus and control dimensions of causality. (29 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2006 APA, all rights reserved). {\textcopyright} 1981 American Psychological Association.},
